<p>W.W. Wood Products published the <strong>Bridged Door</strong> option — a <strong>no-charge modification</strong> in both <a href="/brands/shiloh-cabinetry/">Shiloh</a> and <a href="/brands/eclipse-cabinetry/">Eclipse</a> on base cabinets with full-height doors. It's one of the easiest ways to make a standard cabinet look furniture-built without adding a dollar to the quote.</p>

<h2>What a bridged door is</h2>

<p>A bridged door looks like a <strong>drawer stack</strong>, or a <strong>drawer front over a door</strong>, but operates as <strong>one full-height door</strong>. The client gets the look of drawers with the single-door access they actually need — for plumbing, bulky items, or a clean appliance front.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Available in <strong>Full Overlay / Frameless, Inset, and Modern Inset</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>No upcharge per door.</strong></li>
  <li>Upcoming catalogs will add <strong>three new custom vanity tall cabinets</strong> with bridged doors, and bridged doors will be available on <strong>most cabinet SKUs</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where dealers are using them</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Vanity bases</strong> — single-door access behind what reads as a drawer base. Plumbing stays easy to reach.</li>
  <li><strong>Lower full-height doors on pantries and linen cabinets</strong> — the look of a bank of drawers.</li>
  <li><strong>Dishwasher front panels</strong> — the panel matches the adjacent drawer stack.</li>
  <li><strong>Door-style end panels on base cabinets</strong> — carry the drawer-line rhythm around the end of an island or run.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Spec notes</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Available on <strong>standard vanity and base full-height door cabinets</strong>.</li>
  <li>Doors <strong>up to 24″ wide</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Door and drawer-front styles must match</strong>, with the <strong>700 Edge</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Not available</strong> with <strong>Mitered, Metro, Slab, or Sculpted</strong> doors and drawer fronts.</li>
  <li>For other applications, contact <strong>Shiloh Quotes</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why it matters</h2>

<p>Clients notice drawer lines. A vanity or pantry that reads as stacked drawers looks more custom than a plain door — and with bridged doors that look is free. Make it your default suggestion on vanity sink bases and dishwasher panels.</p>

